<h2 id="where-the-demand-is">Where the side hustle demand is in Baltimore</h2>
<p>Think of Baltimore as a handful of distinct demand zones:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The waterfront arc</strong> — Inner Harbor, Harbor East, Fells Point and Canton — packs restaurants, bars, hotels and young professionals into a few square miles. Great for delivery, rideshare and pet care.</li>
  <li><strong>The medical corridors</strong> around Johns Hopkins Hospital in East Baltimore and the University of Maryland Medical Center downtown, where shift workers keep odd hours and outsource errands.</li>
  <li><strong>Campus neighborhoods</strong> near Johns Hopkins Homewood, Loyola, MICA, the University of Baltimore and Towson University, where students need tutoring, moving help and cheap furniture.</li>
  <li><strong>Leafy north Baltimore and the suburbs</strong> — Roland Park, Homeland, Towson, Catonsville, Ellicott City and Columbia — where bigger homes mean yard work, snow clearing and handyman jobs.</li>
  <li><strong>The stadium district</strong> around Oriole Park at Camden Yards and M&amp;T Bank Stadium, which turns into a rideshare, parking and staffing goldmine on game days.</li>
</ul>
<p>Commuters add another layer: many residents work at Fort Meade, BWI Marshall Airport, the Social Security Administration in Woodlawn or in DC, and they pay for help with everything they don't have time to do.</p>

