<h2 id="by-schedule">Match the gig to your schedule</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Nine-to-five job, weekends free:</strong> pressure washing, handyman work, event staffing and real estate photography all cluster on Saturdays and Sundays.</li>
  <li><strong>Evenings only:</strong> delivery, rideshare to and from the harbor, online tutoring and freelancing fit after-work hours.</li>
  <li><strong>Shift worker with weekdays off:</strong> if you work nights at a hospital or the port, midday dog walks, rental cleaning and Amazon Flex blocks fill your free daylight hours.</li>
  <li><strong>Student:</strong> tutoring, moving help, event staffing and bike delivery around Charles Village and Mount Vernon.</li>
</ul>
<p>The right gig is the one you can protect on your calendar every week. Consistency in month one matters more than chasing the highest hourly rate.</p>

<h2 id="first-month">How to start a side hustle in Baltimore this month</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Pick one gig and one zone.</strong> Dog walking in Canton or pressure washing in Towson beats trying to cover the whole metro.</li>
  <li><strong>Work out your monthly target.</strong> The <a href="/tools/income-calculator/">income calculator</a> turns a dollar goal into weekly hours.</li>
  <li><strong>Get listed.</strong> Gig apps for driving; Nextdoor, neighborhood Facebook groups and a Google Business Profile for local services.</li>
  <li><strong>Collect your first three reviews.</strong> Discount your first jobs if you need to; reviews are your best advertising.</li>
  <li><strong>Mark the surge dates.</strong> Orioles and Ravens home schedules, college move-out weeks and the fall running festival.</li>
  <li><strong>Separate your money.</strong> A dedicated account and a 30% tax set-aside keep April simple.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="maryland-taxes">Maryland and Baltimore City taxes</h2>
<p>Maryland is one of the few states where every resident also pays a local income tax, and Baltimore City's is among the highest.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State income tax:</strong> graduated rates from 2% to 6.5%. For 2026, single filers pay 4.75% on taxable income from about $3,000 to $100,000, and new higher brackets apply only to very high incomes.</li>
  <li><strong>Local income tax:</strong> Baltimore City residents pay 3.2% of taxable income, filed on the same Maryland return. Neighboring counties set their own rates.</li>
  <li><strong>Self-employment tax:</strong> 15.3% federal on net profit, plus federal income tax. Run your numbers with the <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax:</strong> 6% on most goods. Since July 1, 2025, Maryland also taxes certain data and IT services at 3%, which may affect freelance web developers and IT consultants — check the Comptroller's guidance if that's your field.</li>
  <li><strong>Registration:</strong> register a trade name with the state if you don't use your legal name, get a trader's license for retail sales, and remember that LLCs file an annual report with the state.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> Baltimore requires a license for short-term rentals, and many home improvement jobs need a state contractor license. If a job feels bigger than a handyman fix, check before you quote.</div>

